# NEOCOM spec pipeline

Requirement collection is an agent process owned by `/neocom-meta`. L1 epics are authored with `/epic` (and refined in place). There is no `/spec-collect` step and no `02.SOURCES` intake into L1.

```text
/epic  -->  L1 (05.REQUIREMENTS-DECOMPOSITION/L1)
L1          --/neo:extract-l2--> L2/NN.EPIC-NAME.md and/or L2/NN.EPIC-NAME.<group>.md
L2 UNREGISTERED REQUIREMENTS
            --/neo:spec-evolution--> openspec/changes/<system>-<feature>/
                                      specs/<system>/<feature>/spec.md
registered feature
            --apply commits on feature/<feature> from develop-->
            --other features stay open on their own branches-->
            --rebase onto another feature only to use its code-->
            --user reviews and tests-->
            --one pull request into develop can carry several features-->
            --next apply is a new branch from develop-->
            --archive-->  pull request from develop into main
```

## Commands

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `/epic` | Open an epic. The prompt is the name, then the goals. The L1 file is `L1/NN.EPIC-NAME.md`, numbered automatically from `01` |
| `/neocom-methodology` | Design, spec, develop, test, and deploy across repositories. An epic is an L1 file; extract yields epic-named L2 file(s) |
| `/neo:extract-l2` | Convert L1 epics into epic-named L2 files. No args: pending epics; args: only those epics. Skip passages the L2 tree already holds |
| `/neo:spec-evolution` | Review L2 notes and move them into OpenSpec. Optional L2 paths limit the run to those files |
| `/neocom-code` | Implement product code and apply a registered OpenSpec change. Git stays with `/neocom-git` |
| `/feature-delivery` | Apply a registered feature on `feature/<feature>` from `develop` and commit. Several features can be open. Rebase when one needs another's code. One pull request into `develop` can carry several features. Archive opens a pull request from `develop` into `main` |
| `/neocom-git` | Commits, branches, and pull requests. Follow recorded templates under `.cursor/templates/` |
| `/git:commit` | Commit all pending changes on one repository's current branch. `/git:commit <project>` requires a repo name |
| `/git:pr` | Commit pending work and prepare pull requests. `/git:pr <project>` selects one active repository; no project applies to `neocom-meta`, `neocom-front`, and `neocom-back` |

Skills live under `.cursor/skills/`. Slash stubs live under `.cursor/commands/`.

## Systems and features

OpenSpec change names are `<system>-<feature>` (lowercase, numbers, hyphens). The CLI rejects slashes in the change name. The delta spec path is `specs/<system>/<feature>/spec.md`.

Systems: `front`, `infra`, `cicd`, `back`, `domain`, and any later system the user names. The feature is the name being applied, for example `pilot` → change `front-pilot`, branch `feature/pilot`.

## Intake

`/neo:extract-l2` reads L1 only. With no arguments it processes epics that still have pending extract work; with arguments it processes only the named epic(s). It writes new notes under `UNREGISTERED REQUIREMENTS` in epic-named L2 files (`L2/NN.EPIC-NAME.md` or `L2/NN.EPIC-NAME.<group>.md`). It does not require an epic directory or a per-repository split. It treats the current L2 tree as the record of work already generated: those epic-named files, legacy `L2/NN.EPIC-NAME/` directories, `L2/<SYSTEM>/`, `L2/DOMAIN/MODELS/`, and `L2/INFRASTRUCTURE-SPECS.md`. A passage already stated there is reported and not written again.

When an epic register has a **Review** column (EPIC-004 models), extract only rows whose cell is exactly `reviewed`, then set those cells to `extracted`. Leave `to review` sections untouched for a later iteration. Never extract `superseded` or `out of scope`.

`/neo:spec-evolution` reads L2 only. Optional arguments are L2 file paths; when given, only those files are processed. New requirements wait under a heading exactly named `UNREGISTERED REQUIREMENTS` in an L2 file. It does not read L1 to add models that are still `to review`. On processing:

1. Move each item into the file for its system and feature. Create that file when it does not exist.
2. Give it a stable id and leave the `UNREGISTERED REQUIREMENTS` heading empty.
3. Register it in the matching OpenSpec change. Do not invent behavior that the note does not state.
4. Query `neocom-meta/graphify-out/graph.json` for an existing L2 or OpenSpec file that already states the same behavior. Ignore nodes that are not those files. The note text still decides the requirement. When that file already states the same behavior, or a very similar one, move the note under the heading `DUPLICATED` and do not register it.
5. Ignore every note under `DUPLICATED` when processing an L2 file.

## Feature delivery

- Integration branch: `develop`.
- Work branch: `feature/<feature-name>`, created from `develop`, in the repo that owns the code. Several of these branches can be open.
- If `develop` does not exist, stop and ask. Do not create a feature branch from `main` or from another feature branch.
- Rebase onto another open feature only when this feature needs that code. Otherwise rebase onto `develop` before the pull request. Ask before a force push. Do not force-push `develop` or `main`.
- Apply commits on that feature branch, then stops. The user reviews the changes and tests.
- After the user accepts that review, open a pull request into `develop`. Several reviewed features can share one pull request: stack them and open it from the tip. Do not merge it unless the user asks.
- The next apply creates a new `feature/<feature-name>` from `develop`. Do not implement it on the previous feature branch.
- When the OpenSpec change is archived, open a pull request from `develop` into `main` for review and approval. Title it `Release-` plus GitVersion `MajorMinorPatch`. Do not merge it unless the user asks.

## Templates

When the user provides an example commit message, branch name, or pull request body, store it under `neocom-meta/.cursor/templates/` and use that example on later actions. Do not keep a second style beside a recorded example.

## OpenSpec change status register

After **any** OpenSpec change under `neocom-meta/openspec/`, update `openspec/CHANGE-STATUS.md` in the same turn. That includes registering a change, editing proposal/specs/design/tasks, marking tasks done or cancelled, starting or finishing apply, accepting a pull request into `develop`, and archiving.

1. Run `npx openspec list --json` (after `nvm use` in `neocom-meta`) and refresh task counts.
2. Set status (`registered` / `applying` / `applied` / `archived`) and notes to match reality.
3. When a change is archived, move its row to **Archived** and point at the main spec path.
4. Follow the steps already listed in `openspec/CHANGE-STATUS.md`.

Do not leave `CHANGE-STATUS.md` stale after OpenSpec work.

When registering a new change or folding several changes into one feature, refresh `openspec/SPECS-ABSTRACT.md` (why, capabilities, owners, integration clusters) so planners can see the active register at a glance.

## L1 epic traceability

Keep two records in sync so later extract/register runs can **skip** work already processed or applied:

1. `## Spec traceability` on each `L1/NN.EPIC-NAME.md` (status, L2 path, OpenSpec changes, skip note)
2. Central index `L1/TRACEABILITY.md`

Update both after extract, OpenSpec register, apply start, accept into `develop`, and archive. Status values: `none` → `extracted` → `registered` → `applying` → `applied` → `archived`. Passages covered by `applied` or `archived` OpenSpec changes are not re-extracted or re-registered; only new L1 material is processed.

## Rules

- Extract L2 does not write OpenSpec changes and does not place notes in final `L2/<SYSTEM>/` feature registers (except optional epic-named files under a system folder). It does not force per-repo slice files.
- Spec Registration does not read L1 and does not invent requirements.
- Product implementation stays out of `/neo:extract-l2` and `/neo:spec-evolution`.
- When registration or development clarifies architecture or features, update `01.PROGRAMME-DEFINITION/`.
- When OpenSpec artifacts change, update `openspec/CHANGE-STATUS.md` (see above).
